-- title:
--   Second inequality at prime degree, no roots of unity assumed
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a number field, $E$ a number field which is a $k$-algebra, $p$ a prime with $\operatorname{finrank}_k E = p$, and suppose $E/k$ is Galois. Assume the following uniform idelic first-inequality hypothesis: for every pair of number fields $L$, $M$ in the universe of $E$ with $M$ an $L$-algebra, $M/L$ Galois of degree $p$, and every ideal $\mathfrak f_0 \subseteq \mathcal O_L$ with $\mathfrak f_0 \neq 0$ such that $v^{\,2e_v+1} \mid \mathfrak f_0$ for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_L$ whose chosen prime above in $M$ has non-trivial inertia subgroup in $\operatorname{Aut}_L(M)$, where $e_v$ is the ramification index of $(p)$ at $v$, the predicate `IdeleFirstIneqDataAt L M 𝔣₀` holds: there is a monoid homomorphism $\mathrm{Nrm}$ from the idele units of $M$ to those of $L$ which carries adjusters at $\mathfrak f_0\mathcal O_M$ to adjusters at $\mathfrak f_0$ along $\alpha \mapsto N_{M/L}(\alpha)$, whose effect on the content of the finite part is the relative-norm map `fracRelNormUnit` on fractional-ideal units, whose range contains all unit ideles at $\mathfrak f_0$ whenever $\mathfrak f_0$ satisfies `IsAdmissibleModulus L M`, and for which $[M:L]$ divides the index of the subgroup generated by the principal ideles and the range of $\mathrm{Nrm}$. Then for every ideal $\mathfrak f \subseteq \mathcal O_k$ the index, inside the group of fractional ideals of $k$ coprime to $\mathfrak f$, of the subgroup generated by the narrow ray subgroup at $\mathfrak f$ together with the image of the relative norm from $E$, divides $\#\operatorname{Aut}_k(E)$.
--
--   This is the second inequality of global class field theory in its ideal-theoretic form, for a Galois extension of prime degree, with no assumption that the base contains the $p$-th roots of unity; it differs from [`NumberField.PrimeNormIndex.secondInequalityCTM_of_primitiveRoots`](thm.html#NumberField.PrimeNormIndex.secondInequalityCTM_of_primitiveRoots) in dropping that assumption while strengthening the idelic first-inequality hypothesis to hold over all base fields of the relevant universe rather than over $E$ alone. It feeds the prime-power and idele-class formulations used later, namely [`M4aKummer.normClassIndex_dvd_card_aut_of_prime_pow`](thm.html#M4aKummer.normClassIndex_dvd_card_aut_of_prime_pow) and [`NumberField.PrimeNormIndex.ideleClass_normCoset_index_dvd_of_finrank_eq_prime`](thm.html#NumberField.PrimeNormIndex.ideleClass_normCoset_index_dvd_of_finrank_eq_prime).
